WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:05.080
One command, run as me
against a fresh environment.

2
00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:11.240
Two workflow runs passed, and Storage now
holds a record whose createUser is me.

3
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.400
Not the platform's identity.

4
00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:16.600
Mine. Here's how it got there.

5
00:00:23.524 --> 00:00:25.314
Fieldnote eleven.

6
00:00:25.314 --> 00:00:26.764
On behalf of you.

7
00:00:27.832 --> 00:00:32.002
How does a DAG reach the stack,
who does each task act as,

8
00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:34.562
and how do you know it stored anything?

9
00:00:35.954 --> 00:00:40.044
In the core profile, Airflow runs
the community's ingestion DAGs.

10
00:00:40.354 --> 00:00:44.354
The stack decides which files
load, registers them with Workflow,

11
00:00:44.354 --> 00:00:47.724
and runs each task on behalf
of whoever started it.

12
00:00:47.954 --> 00:00:50.124
Then one command proves the whole path.

13
00:00:51.874 --> 00:00:54.274
The stack's catalog names two families:

14
00:00:54.274 --> 00:00:58.274
its own smoke DAGs, and the
community's manifest ingestion,

15
00:00:58.274 --> 00:01:02.364
pinned to a full commit with
a SHA-256 for every file.

16
00:01:02.994 --> 00:01:06.204
At each scheduler start, a
loader fetches that commit,

17
00:01:06.274 --> 00:01:10.444
checks every hash, and stages
only the files the catalog lists.

18
00:01:10.994 --> 00:01:14.114
The Python libraries
install hash-locked too.

19
00:01:14.114 --> 00:01:17.154
A file that doesn't match
holds the new pod in init,

20
00:01:17.154 --> 00:01:19.084
while the old pod keeps serving.

21
00:01:19.634 --> 00:01:22.124
So a catalog edit approves code to run.

22
00:01:24.582 --> 00:01:28.992
After Airflow and init, Flux's
sixth layer runs a registration Job.

23
00:01:29.302 --> 00:01:33.152
It waits until Airflow has parsed
exactly the declared DAGs,

24
00:01:33.222 --> 00:01:38.662
with no import errors, registers each
one with Workflow as a system workflow,

25
00:01:38.662 --> 00:01:40.112
and unpauses it.

26
00:01:40.262 --> 00:01:41.872
It never deletes anything.

27
00:01:42.262 --> 00:01:46.782
That layer does not gate Deployable, so an
environment can be ready to deploy before

28
00:01:46.782 --> 00:01:48.512
ingestion is ready to run.

29
00:01:51.026 --> 00:01:52.396
Follow who acts.

30
00:01:52.546 --> 00:01:54.956
You call Workflow with your own token.

31
00:01:55.346 --> 00:02:00.396
Workflow records you as the submitter,
and refuses a user id in the request.

32
00:02:00.546 --> 00:02:05.436
It reaches Airflow with its own login,
which carries no OSDU authority.

33
00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:10.156
The task then calls Storage
as the stack's OSDU identity,

34
00:02:10.306 --> 00:02:12.636
with an on-behalf-of header naming you.

35
00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:17.586
Entitlements honours that only when the
identity may delegate and you may be

36
00:02:17.586 --> 00:02:22.076
impersonated. spi users
gives that right to editors,

37
00:02:22.186 --> 00:02:24.636
admins and ops, not viewers.

38
00:02:24.866 --> 00:02:29.036
So the stored record's createUser
is you, not the platform.

39
00:02:31.558 --> 00:02:32.888
One command proves it.

40
00:02:33.198 --> 00:02:35.288
spi test dags --as me.

41
00:02:35.758 --> 00:02:38.078
Preflight checks that
you may act for others,

42
00:02:38.078 --> 00:02:41.438
that both DAGs are registered,
that the schemas exist,

43
00:02:41.438 --> 00:02:46.168
that the legal tag is valid, and that
the record's ACL matches your domain.

44
00:02:46.558 --> 00:02:51.128
Then Echo_DAG runs, and then
Osdu_ingest with a one-record manifest.

45
00:02:51.518 --> 00:02:56.328
Last, it reads the record back from
Storage and checks that createUser is you.

46
00:02:56.798 --> 00:03:00.728
With --report, the evidence
lands on one page.

47
00:03:03.818 --> 00:03:04.978
An easy mistake:

48
00:03:04.978 --> 00:03:07.788
the run finished, so
every record is stored.

49
00:03:08.178 --> 00:03:10.818
When Storage refuses every
record in a manifest,

50
00:03:10.818 --> 00:03:13.548
Osdu_ingest fails the run.

51
00:03:13.818 --> 00:03:17.068
When it refuses only some,
the run still finishes.

52
00:03:17.218 --> 00:03:21.868
Here, two records went in, and the
second had an ACL in another domain.

53
00:03:22.018 --> 00:03:23.468
The run finished.

54
00:03:23.618 --> 00:03:25.388
The first record reads back.

55
00:03:25.458 --> 00:03:30.098
The second is not found, and the only trace
is a skipped-entities report in the task

56
00:03:30.098 --> 00:03:34.588
log. So read back every record
you sent, not the run's state.

57
00:03:34.858 --> 00:03:40.578
That's also why the check refuses a bad
legal tag or a foreign ACL before it submits

58
00:03:40.578 --> 00:03:41.468
anything.

59
00:03:43.966 --> 00:03:45.256
So now you can say:

60
00:03:45.406 --> 00:03:48.366
a DAG reaches Airflow
from a reviewed catalog,

61
00:03:48.366 --> 00:03:53.416
hash by hash. Registration comes
later, and does not gate Deployable.

62
00:03:53.726 --> 00:03:56.296
And each task writes on
behalf of the caller.

63
00:03:58.662 --> 00:04:01.872
Next: how do you prove a
service from your desk?

